---
import CalculatorLayout from '@layouts/CalculatorLayout.astro';
import IdealWeightCalculator from '@components/islands/IdealWeightCalculator.astro';
import { getCalculator } from '@data/calculators';

const calc = getCalculator('health', 'ideal-weight-calculator')!;

const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'Which ideal weight formula is best?',
    answer:
      'There is no single "correct" formula. The Robinson, Miller, Devine and Hamwi equations were each developed for different purposes and give slightly different results. Treat the spread of values as a healthy range rather than one exact target.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How is ideal body weight calculated?',
    answer:
      'Each formula starts from a base weight at 5 feet of height and adds a fixed amount per inch above that. The Devine formula, for example, is 50 kg + 2.3 kg per inch over 5 ft for men (45.5 kg for women), so a 5\'10" man works out to about 73 kg (161 lb).',
  },
  {
    question: 'Why do the formulas only use height and sex?',
    answer:
      'These classic formulas estimate a reference weight from height and sex alone. They do not account for body frame, muscle mass or age, which is why the healthy-BMI range is also shown for context.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How much should I weigh for my height?',
    answer:
      'Rather than one number, aim for the range that corresponds to a healthy BMI of 18.5–24.9 for your height, which the calculator shows alongside the formula estimates. A wider frame or more muscle can place a healthy weight toward or above the top of that range.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Is ideal weight the same as a healthy weight?',
    answer:
      'Not exactly. "Ideal body weight" formulas were originally created for medication dosing and clinical use. A healthy weight is better described as a range — which is why we also show the weights corresponding to a BMI of 18.5–24.9.',
  },
];
---

<CalculatorLayout
  calculator={calc}
  presentation="task-first"
  intro="Estimate your ideal body weight from height and sex using four established formulas (Robinson, Miller, Devine, Hamwi), plus the healthy weight range for your height."
  faqs={faqs}
  reviewMetadata={{ reviewedDate: '2026-07-18' }}
>
  <IdealWeightCalculator />

  <Fragment slot="content">
    <h2>Reading the results</h2>
    <p>
      Because the four formulas were derived differently, they produce a range of values
      rather than one number. A realistic, healthy target usually sits somewhere within
      that range and within the BMI-based healthy range shown alongside it.
    </p>

    <h2>How the formulas work</h2>
    <p>
      Each equation sets a base weight for a 5-foot frame and adds a set amount for every
      inch above that. They differ only in those constants — which is why, for a 5'10" man,
      Devine gives about 73 kg while the others land a kilogram or two either side. Seeing
      all four at once is more honest than pretending any single one is exact.
    </p>

    <h2>Beyond the formulas</h2>
    <p>
      Frame size, muscle mass, age and overall health all affect what a healthy weight
      looks like for you. Athletes in particular may weigh more than these formulas
      suggest while being very healthy. For the weight range tied to a healthy BMI, see the
      <a href="/health/bmi-calculator">BMI calculator</a>. Use the results as a guide, not a rule.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is general educational information, not medical advice. Talk to a healthcare
      professional about a weight target that is right for you.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</CalculatorLayout>
